Short:        Trims a recording's ends by eye and ear
Uploader:     marcus.gerards@googlemail.com (Marcus Gerards)
Author:       Marcus Gerards
Type:         mus/edit
Version:      @VERSION@
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.2.0
Distribution: NoCD
Requires:     AmigaOS 3.2; driver/audio/ahiusr_4.18.lha or Prisma Megamix card
Kurz:         Kuerzt die Enden einer Aufnahme

WaveSmith @VERSION@  --  @DATE@


WHAT IT IS

WaveSmith opens a recording -- a record side, a tape, a radio show taken
with a sampler or a sound card -- finds where the music starts and where
it ends, and shows the lead-in and the run-out shaded, In and Out set
before and after them. Listen to both ends, drag the handles if the guess
was not right, and Save: the file is written anew without the shaded
parts.

It also cuts parts out, splits a side into its tracks at markers and
writes a cue sheet for them, and puts fades and gain on what it keeps.


WHAT IT DOES

  * the suggestion: the lead-in's groove noise and clicks, the run-out
    after the last note, a margin before and after the music, every click
    counted -- a long file's ends are read first, so In and Out are there
    before the rest of the picture
  * the window: an overview of the whole file, the waveform zoomed down
    to single samples, one bar under it with the deck, Loop, the flags In
    and Out, the length, Trim... and the lenses; a bubble says what is
    under the mouse pointer
  * the Trim window: the handles' times typed or nudged, each end listened
    to, a fade on each
  * listening: Play and Pause, Play selection, Listen at In and at Out,
    Loop; the play head a sprite, steady on slow machines; the controls
    answered first while the picture fills in by pieces (an A2000 with a
    25 MHz 68030 keeps up)
  * editing: Cut out, Undo and Redo, Split at the cursor; markers, Find
    tracks, Split at markers, a cue sheet beside the files
  * effects: Fade in, Fade out, fades at In and Out that follow the
    handles, Gain..., Normalise, all drawn in the waveform
  * Save, Save as, Open next; a backup of the original if wanted, an icon
    for the new file if wanted
  * English and German, an AmigaGuide manual in both, the Help key opens
    the page about what is under the mouse pointer

The sound stays in the file while WaveSmith works on it; only its picture
is kept in memory, so a whole record side of 25 minutes is no trouble.


WHAT IT NEEDS

  * AmigaOS 3.2 (it is built with ReAction).
  * To listen: AHI (ahi.device 4 or newer), or a Prisma Megamix sound card
    with its software (prisma.library 3 or newer). Without either,
    WaveSmith says so when it starts.
  * WAV files, 16-bit PCM, mono or stereo, are read as they are. AIFF,
    FLAC, MP3, Ogg Vorbis and AAC are decoded on the Prisma Megamix card
    (prismadsp.library 1.1 or newer).
  * Any 680x0; a 68030 or better is recommended. Tested on 68030s at 25
    and 50 MHz (an A2000 and an A1200) and on a 68060.
  * A screen of 640 x 256 or more; a deep one shows Mason's GlowIcons and
    the About picture in their colours.


INSTALLATION

Double-click Install. WaveSmith goes into a drawer of its own, SYS:Tools
by default, with its manual (Help) and its German translation
(Catalogs). Nothing goes into LIBS:, DEVS: or S:.

  * Novice: SYS:Tools/WaveSmith, with the GlowIcons.
  * Intermediate and Expert: it asks where, and which icons -- the
    GlowIcons or the 4-colour classic ones.

By hand: copy the drawer Files/WaveSmith wherever you like, and
Files/WaveSmith.info beside it for its icon. For the GlowIcons, copy what
is in Files/Icons/Glow/WaveSmith over that drawer, and use
Files/Icons/Glow/WaveSmithDrawer.info as the drawer's icon.

To open a sound file by double-clicking its icon, make WaveSmith the icon's
default tool, or click WaveSmith's icon with the file's icon shift-clicked.
From the Shell:

  WaveSmith [FILE] <file> [PUBSCREEN <name>]


HISTORY

1.0  (@DATE@)
     - first public release, with an Installer and a drawer of its own
     - free software under the LGPL 2.1 or later, the source on GitHub
     - the About window says what WaveSmith does
0.4  The new name (it was PrismaEdit); the bar, the Trim window, a steady
     play head, the controls first on slow machines; Mason's art.
0.3  AHI, German, the manual and the Help key, fades and gain.
0.2  The card, files other than WAV, Cut out, Undo, markers, cue sheets.
0.1  First version: In and Out suggested, trimmed, saved.


